#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# mathieu_characteristic_a

#% text
# `mathieu_characteristic_a(r, q)` gives the characteristic value `a_r(q)` of the even Mathieu functions.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# At an integer `r >= 0` the even solution is periodic (period `pi` for even `r`, `2 pi` for odd `r`); for other real `r` the value has characteristic exponent `r`, `cos(pi r) = w_I(pi; a, q)` (DLMF 28.2.16), continuous in `r` with `a_r(0) = r^2`; `a_(-r) = a_r`.

#% item
# Numerical values for real `q`: the eigenvalues of the truncated tridiagonal matrices of the Fourier coefficients (DLMF 28.4) as a start, refined by the secant method on `y'(pi/2)` or `y(pi/2)` (integer `r`) or `w_I(pi) - cos(pi r)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
mathieu_characteristic_a(1, 0.5)
#% output
#= 1.4667668425160558

#% cell
mathieu_characteristic_a([1, 2], 0.33)
#% output
#= [1.3158195407174649, 4.044736208026365]

#% cell
mathieu_characteristic_a(15/17, 0.5)
#% output
#= 0.43084814223083295

#% cell
mathieu_characteristic_a(-2*pi, 0.5)
#% output
#= 39.48166637624438

#% cell
mathieu_characteristic_a(r, 0)
#% output
#= r^2

